    s__TALKER_UNDER_TWO(py_str("By eighteen months most children say six to twenty words and point at what they want, so a handful at that age is on track. No words at all, or words they had and have stopped using, is worth a hearing test rather than waiting: ring the health visitor and say what you have noticed, and keep naming what they point at so the word is there when they reach for it."));
    s__TALKER_TWO(py_str("By two most children have around fifty words and are joining two together ('more juice', 'daddy gone'), and it is the joining that matters as much as the count. A two year old with only a handful of words is worth a hearing test first - glue ear is the commonest and most fixable reason - and ask the health visitor for a speech and language referral rather than waiting, because the waiting list is the thing to beat. Read the same book every night and give them a word for what they point at."));
    s__TALKER_THREE(py_str("By three, most children say short sentences and a stranger understands half of what they say, so a three-year-old with only a handful of words is worth a hearing test first - glue ear is the commonest and most fixable reason. Ask your health visitor or GP for a referral to speech and language therapy rather than waiting: the waiting list is the thing to beat, and the help is play-based and free. Keep talking, reading and narrating the day meanwhile, and answer what they point at so the words come easier."));
    s__TALKER_SCHOOL(py_str("By four a child should be talking in sentences a stranger understands, so a four year old with only a handful of words needs a hearing test and a referral now rather than a wait: ask the GP for speech and language therapy, and tell the nursery or school so the SENCO can start support while you wait. Read together every day and give them time to find the word - the pause is usually the idea, not the answer."));
    s__TALKER_HELP(py_str("Talk at them all day about what you are both looking at, in short sentences, and leave a pause after each one so they get a turn - the pause is the part parents skip. Name what they point at, read the same few books until they join in, sing the same songs, and keep the dummy for sleep only, because a mouth full of dummy has no room for words."));
